# What: Project-specific firewall allowlist additions, read at container
#       start by init-firewall.sh.
# Why:  init-firewall.sh itself is [locked] in kit/manifest.txt — the
#       devshop overwrites the workspace copy with the template version
#       on every container start, so edits to init-firewall.sh's static
#       list silently revert. This file is [scaffold-once]: the template
#       seeds it once, then the project owns it. init-devshop.sh chmods
#       it root:root 644 on every start so node (Claude / npm) cannot
#       modify; only the operator with `sudo nano` can edit.
# Does NOT: Replace the static template list in init-firewall.sh (which
#       covers npm / GitHub / Anthropic / Sentry / Vercel / VS Code /
#       common CDNs). Add ONLY project-specific external services here:
#       your Supabase project URL, Sentry org ingest, PostHog region,
#       Vercel app URL — anything that varies per project.
#
# Format:
#   - One bare hostname per line (no scheme, no path, no port).
#   - Lines starting with `#` and blank lines are ignored.
#   - Each line is validated against a strict DNS-label regex before
#     it's resolved + added to the firewall ipset. Malformed entries
#     are logged and skipped, not silently included.
#
# Examples (uncomment + customize per project):
#   <ref>.supabase.co                           — your Supabase project URL
#   api.supabase.com                            — Supabase control plane (needed for `supabase link`)
#   o<org-id>.ingest.us.sentry.io               — your Sentry org ingest (or .eu.sentry.io)
#   us.i.posthog.com                            — PostHog ingest (us or eu)
#   us-assets.i.posthog.com                     — PostHog client assets (us or eu)
#   <your-app>.vercel.app                       — your Vercel production URL
#
# Pre-seeded (do NOT remove unless you replace next/font/google with another
# font source). next/font/google fetches these at request time; without
# them, the dev server hangs serving the first request (the font fetch
# never returns) and exits silently with no error logged. Symptom:
# `next dev` produces "✓ Ready in <N>ms" then dies mid-request with only
# `[?25h` (cursor-show terminal escape) as final output. Took multiple
# debug cycles on home-os PR #15 to trace.
fonts.googleapis.com
fonts.gstatic.com
#
# After editing this file, additions take effect on next container rebuild.
#
# In-session shortcut WITHOUT rebuild — additive `ipset add` (same
# mechanism as refresh-allowed-ips.sh; safe mid-session, no flush):
#     dig +short A <hostname> | while read ip; do
#       sudo ipset add allowed-domains "$ip" 2>/dev/null || true
#     done
#
# DON'T re-run `sudo /usr/local/bin/init-firewall.sh` to refresh —
# observed twice (2026-04-20, pre-refresh-allowed-ips.sh) to stall
# the Claude session. The triggering use-case (refresh stale prod-URL
# IPs) no longer applies, but the underlying iptables/mangle flush +
# ipset destroy code path is unchanged so the conntrack-invalidation
# risk plausibly remains. With both current refresh use-cases now
# additive-only (refresh-allowed-ips.sh + the ipset add shortcut
# above), there's no reason to invoke init-firewall.sh mid-session
# anyway. See kit/TODOS.md "init-firewall.sh hangs the Claude Code
# session" + kit/docs/guide/ops.md § Known Footguns § Firewall IP
# rotation.
